<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Caucasus</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Kakheti</h1><p class="st-meta">786 CE – 1010 CE (approx.) · capital Telavi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">An eastern Georgian principality ruled by chorepiscopi, leaders with a churchly title, that resisted the Arab emirs of Tbilisi and the Bagratid kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti">Kakheti</a>, the land east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tbilisi">Tbilisi</a>, broke away from the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-iberia">Principality of Iberia</a> in 786, when the nobleman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grigol-of-kakheti">Grigol</a> took the title chorepiscopus and united its mountain communities. The weakening of central power under Arab rule allowed it to become a separate state.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Its rulers, whose title meant rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> although they were lay princes, ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ujarma">Ujarma</a> and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/telavi">Telavi</a> with the support of the mountain peoples of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tusheti">Tusheti</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pshavi">Pshavi</a>. They fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/emirate-of-tbilisi">Emirate of Tbilisi</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-abkhazia">Kingdom of Abkhazia</a>, and in the 10th century absorbed the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-hereti">Principality of Hereti</a>. Viticulture and farming in the Alazani valley sustained it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Around 1010 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kvirike-iii-of-kakheti">Kvirike III</a> took the title king, forming the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti-hereti">Kingdom of Kakheti-Hereti</a>, which held out against the united Georgian monarchy until 1104. <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-kakheti">Kakheti</a> later re-emerged as a separate Georgian kingdom in the 15th century.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-kakheti.jpg" alt="Principality of Kakheti" width="320" height="248"><figcaption>Image: Don-kun · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caucasus_900_map_alt_de.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>786 CE – 1010 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Telavi</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Grigol, Kvirike I, Kvirike III</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Georgian Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Georgian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/georgia">Georgia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Chorepiscopate of Kakheti, Kakheti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=898&amp;focus=principality-of-kakheti" aria-label="Principality of Kakheti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5786 685 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Kakheti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6106 941L6091 947M6106 941L6106 941"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6091 947h0M6106 941h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6106 941h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6106 941h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Telavi. <a href="/atlas?year=898&amp;focus=principality-of-kakheti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
